    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "Since 3.3.0, the declared item type of a `dict[str, str]` is used to type the result of `.get(key)` as `str`. When the key is missing, `.get()` returns `None`, but the generated code applies the `str` fast paths to it without a `None` check. Truthiness silently returns the wrong answer, and `len()` reads out of bounds.\n\n## Reproducer\n\n```python\n# g.py\ndef truth(d: dict[str, str]):\n    v = d.get('missing')\n    return bool(v)\n\n  def length(d: dict[str, str]):\n      v = d.get('missing')\n      return len(v)\n\ndef contains(d: dict[str, str]):\n    v = d.get('missing')\n    return 'a' in v\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The bug affects not only missing keys but also `None` values:\n> ```python\n> def length(d: dict[str, str]):\n>    v = d.get('none')\n>    return len(v)  \n> length({'none': None})\n> ```\n\n```python\n# setup.py\nfrom setuptools import setup, Extension\nfrom Cython.Build import cythonize\n\nsetup(name='g', ext_modules=cythonize([Extension(\"g\", [\"g.py\"])],\n      compiler_directives={\"language_level\": 3}))\n```\n\nCalled as `f({'a': 'b'})`, so the key is missing and `.get()` returns `None`:\n\n| | CPython | Cython 3.2.5 | Cython 3.3.0 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `bool(v)` | `False` | `False` | **`True`** |\n| `len(v)` | `TypeError` | `TypeError` | **`4294967295`** |\n| `'a' in v` | `TypeError` | `TypeError` | **`True`** |\n\n`v == 'a'` and `v + 'a'` behave correctly.\n\n## Generated C\n\n3.3.0, for `bool(v)` — no `None` check:\n\n```c\nPy_ssize_t __pyx_temp = __Pyx_PyUnicode_IS_TRUE(__pyx_v_v);\n```\n\n3.2.5, same line:\n\n```c\nif (__pyx_v_v == Py_None) __pyx_t_1 = 0;\nelse {\n  Py_ssize_t __pyx_temp = __Pyx_PyUnicode_IS_TRUE(__pyx_v_v);\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThe assignment itself explicitly permits `None`:\n\n```c\nif (!(likely(PyUnicode_CheckExact(__pyx_t_1))||((__pyx_t_1) == Py_None) || __Pyx_RaiseUnexpectedTypeError(\"str\", __pyx_t_1))) ...\n```\n\nso nothing raises there — only the later fast paths assume a real `str`.\n\n## Notes\n\n* Independent of `infer_types`; reproduces with and without it.\n* Annotating `v: str | None = d.get('missing')` does not help, and neither does passing the default explicitly as `d.get('missing', None)`.\n* Dropping the item type (`d: dict`) avoids it, as does an explicit `v is not None and v`.\n* Presumably comes from \"Declared container item types are now used by the type system\" (#7288, #7798).\n\n## Environment\n\nCython 3.3.0 (vs 3.2.5), CPython 3.12.3, Linux x86_64 (also observed on aarch64), gcc 15.\n\nThis hit us in production: a value read with `.get()` from a `dict[str, str] | None` passed an `if v` guard while being `None`, so `None` flowed into code that could not receive it.\n",
