            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "i confirm the bug. But I am puzzled what is purpose of the test `__pyx_t_1 == Py_None` in line:\n```c\nif (!(likely(PyUnicode_CheckExact(__pyx_t_1))||((__pyx_t_1) == Py_None) || __Pyx_RaiseUnexpectedTypeError(\"str\", __pyx_t_1))) __PYX_ERR(0, 4, __pyx_L1_error)\n```\n~~It should not guard against `None` value? And if it is not guarding what is the purpose of that expression?~~ Update: The condition just allows None and str values... Now it is clear for me the purpouse of the test...\n\nAlso it is interesting that annotating variable instead it adds correct `None` guard:\n```cython\ndef main(d: dict):\n    v: str = d.get('missing')\n    return len(v)\n``` \nProduces the same code just adds extra None guard:\n```c\n   if (unlikely(__pyx_v_v == Py_None)) {\n     PyErr_SetString(PyExc_TypeError, \"object of type \\047NoneType\\047 has no len()\");\n     __PYX_ERR(0, 5, __pyx_L1_error)\n   }\n   __pyx_t_2 = __Pyx_PyUnicode_GET_LENGTH(__pyx_v_v); if (unlikely(__pyx_t_2 == ((Py_ssize_t)-1))) __PYX_ERR(0, 5, __pyx_L1_error)\n   __pyx_t_1 = PyLong_FromSsize_t(__pyx_t_2); if (unlikely(!__pyx_t_1)) __PYX_ERR(0, 5, __pyx_L1_error)\n   __Pyx_GOTREF(__pyx_t_1);\n```\n\nHence two points are unclear for me:\n* ~~What is the purpose of the first `Py_None` equality test~~ Update, now I understand that code it basically allows `str` or `None` values ...\n* Why plain `str` variable annotation adds second (correct) guard but inference to the same type (`str`) does not.",
